I've finished T221. report.md and notes.md are in `child/`. The 14-mark problem is ill-posed as stated, so I solved it on the 11 independent marks, which departs from the literal statement.

**The problem with the statement:** C(TRAIN) is exactly singular, with rank 11 of 14. B.L_no00, L_no10 and L_no01 are exact duplicates of B.g00, g10 and g01. D vanishes on the same three null directions, so the eigenproblem is 0/0 there.

**What I did about it:** I dropped the three duplicate marks and set w to 0 on them. Adding null vectors to w changes neither lambda nor q, and I checked that.

**Results:**
- **Control:** 0.929632.
- **lambda_min:** 0.02691319.
- **w:** B.g00 −0.568319, g10 −0.805731, g01 −0.546735, g11 0.049551, Rv −0.517439, Rh −0.630043, L_no00 0, L_no10 0, L_no01 0, L_no11 −0.811886, n00 1, n10 0.661133, n01 0.924056, n11 0.122737.
- **q on HELD:** 0.02674971 ± 0.00069346 (jackknife, n = 16).
- **Smallest single-mark quotient on HELD:** B.Rh, 0.04807613.
- **cond(C(TRAIN)):** numerically 2.58e17 on the full 14×14, where the true value is infinite. On the reduced 11×11 it is 43.44.

